estaurant from  delivering pizzas and I saw guys in the  kitchen and they were just throwing  things together like there was clearly  no  recipe there was clearly no rhyme or  reason they're just throwing things  together  and it kind of struck me is I previously  thought everyone just followed a recipe  right right and I realized wow you have  the ability to just create whatever you  want and no one's gonna tell you that  it's wrong or right necessarily you can  just have that level of creativity and I  think I was hooked you ended up getting  a job in a restaurant in Charlottesville  which is if you've said is kind of was  kind of your cooking school yeah there's  a there was a wonderful little  restaurant in Charlottesville called  oXXO and a guy named John Haywood who  was the chef/owner and he kind of agreed  to take me on as you know essentially an  apprentice and I worked up through the  ranks there and that acted is  essentially my culinary school but  tenacity is your calling card right I  mean you use that to get a gig with Sean  Brock the great celebrated southern chef  tell us how that happened I didn't  realize this I now fully understand how  annoying it must have been seven 2008  when the economy wasn't so good I was  going down and knocking on their door  saying hire me hire me hire me hire me  and I just didn't give up and at the  time they weren't you know not only were  they not hiring but they were having to  lay people off and it was a tough time  but I just kept showing back up you got  a tip about how what to do exactly Tim I  befriended the chef's there and one of  them called me and said hey we're we're  down to ours pretty much our skeleton  crew and there's a guy who's going to be  leaving yeah so I bet if you were to  come down here and be here the same day  that he puts in his notice  hot tip yeah Sean would probably hire  you and luckily it worked out Wow just  looked at you and said you want the job  with your goal always to open your 